  "live_records": {
    "q-38244": {
      "dom_id": "step-q38244",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2u+v) \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2u+v)$ and $y=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{2}{2u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{2u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{2u+v} & \\frac{1}{2u+v} \\\\ \\,\\text{ }v\\, & \\,\\text{ }u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2u+v}\\cdot u-\\frac{1}{2u+v}\\cdot v \\\\  & =\\frac{2u-v}{2u+v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
